who have to deal with my excitement and energy to make things even better.

37
00:02:22,800 --> 00:02:24,800
Thank you for all you've done for scouts like me.

38
00:02:24,800 --> 00:02:25,800
Keep up the great work.

39
00:02:25,800 --> 00:02:27,800
Well, thank you, Steven.

40
00:02:27,800 --> 00:02:31,800
It's always good to hear that people find what we're doing here useful.

41
00:02:31,800 --> 00:02:35,800
Craig Snodgrass is from Troop 1 in Altadena, California,

42
00:02:35,800 --> 00:02:36,800
where he is the scout master.

43
00:02:36,800 --> 00:02:40,800
Craig just checked in to kind of send me a report

44
00:02:40,800 --> 00:02:45,800
about the way that their troop is incorporating the quarterly troop plan.

45
00:02:45,800 --> 00:02:48,800
I've talked about that in a past podcast and a blog post

46
00:02:48,800 --> 00:02:52,800
and I'll refer to those in the podcast notes.

47
00:02:52,800 --> 00:02:54,800
But anyway, Craig wrote to say this:

48
00:02:54,800 --> 00:02:57,800
I've been so happy with your 8-Part Fundamental Series

49
00:02:57,800 --> 00:02:59,800
that I've shared it widely with others.

50
00:02:59,800 --> 00:03:02,800
I've listened to each of the 8 podcasts at least twice

51
00:03:02,800 --> 00:03:05,800
and found new insights and understanding. each time.

52
00:03:05,800 --> 00:03:06,800
They're a masterpiece.

53
00:03:06,800 --> 00:03:08,800
Well, thank you, Craig.

54
00:03:08,800 --> 00:03:10,800
I'm humbled.

55
00:03:10,800 --> 00:03:14,800
Our parents love having a quarterly parents meeting.

56
00:03:14,800 --> 00:03:16,800
We've always had quarterly course of honor

57
00:03:16,800 --> 00:03:19,800
and we had the intention of holding some kind of parents meeting

58
00:03:19,800 --> 00:03:21,800
but never quite got around to it.

59
00:03:21,800 --> 00:03:24,800
So having it just before the quarterly court of honor

60
00:03:24,800 --> 00:03:26,800
has worked out perfectly for us.

61
00:03:26,800 --> 00:03:29,800
Our Petroleum Leaders Council adopted the three month terms

62
00:03:29,800 --> 00:03:33,800
for positions of responsibility you described in the quarterly plan.

63
00:03:33,800 --> 00:03:36,800
We continue to make the change to quarterly planning

64
00:03:36,800 --> 00:03:38,800
with a Petroleum Leaders Council

65
00:03:38,800 --> 00:03:40,800
meaning devoted to that every three months,

66
00:03:40,800 --> 00:03:43,800
And this avoids having the annual planning marathon

67
00:03:43,800 --> 00:03:45,800
wrapped. everybody a little burned out

68
00:03:45,800 --> 00:03:48,800
And we're in various stages of adopting the idea

69
00:03:48,800 --> 00:03:51,800
of the three month permission slip and payments.

70
00:03:51,800 --> 00:03:53,800
But I just want to check in and let you know

71
00:03:53,800 --> 00:03:55,800
that, as a Scout Master, I'm really buying in

72
00:03:55,800 --> 00:03:59,800
and it's all appearing to work. so far, so good.

73
00:03:59,800 --> 00:04:00,800
Well, thanks, Craig.

74
00:04:00,800 --> 00:04:02,800
I'm glad that that's working out for you.

75
00:04:02,800 --> 00:04:06,800
As I said, I'll reference the material about that plan

76
00:04:06,800 --> 00:04:08,800
in the podcast notes.

77
00:04:08,800 --> 00:04:09,800
Take a look at it

78
00:04:09,800 --> 00:04:11,800
And if you've been working with that,

79
00:04:11,800 --> 00:04:13,800
if you've been adapting that approach for your troop,

80
00:04:13,800 --> 00:04:15,800
I'd like to hear how it's working.

81
00:04:15,800 --> 00:04:17,800
Well. the remainder of the podcast,

82
00:04:17,800 --> 00:04:20,800
I'm going to share some answers to email questions

83
00:04:20,800 --> 00:04:22,800
I have recently received.

84
00:04:22,800 --> 00:04:24,800
So let's get started. shall we,

85
00:04:24,800 --> 00:04:27,800
Stepping to my forest,

86
00:04:27,800 --> 00:04:31,800
Feel the magic in the air?

87
00:04:31,800 --> 00:04:34,800
I know that you're not evil.

88
00:04:34,800 --> 00:04:38,800
I know deep down you care.

89
00:04:38,800 --> 00:04:41,800
I cannot help but wonder

90
00:04:41,800 --> 00:04:45,800
Why you do the things you do.

91
00:04:45,800 --> 00:04:48,800
I hope you will remember

92
00:04:48,800 --> 00:04:53,800
That it's applied it to,

93
00:04:53,800 --> 00:05:00,800
Cause I am a Sasquatch

94
00:05:00,800 --> 00:05:07,800
And I live among the trees

95
00:05:07,800 --> 00:05:14,800
And I am proud to be a Sasquatch.

96
00:05:14,800 --> 00:05:20,800
I live in peace and harmony,

97
00:05:20,800 --> 00:05:23,800
In harmony.

98
00:05:23,800 --> 00:05:27,800
Tim Nelson is with Cub Scout Pack 352

99
00:05:27,800 --> 00:05:29,800
in Raleigh, North Carolina,

100
00:05:29,800 --> 00:05:31,800
And Tim wrote in to say:

101
00:05:31,800 --> 00:05:33,800
I'm the den leader of ten bears

102
00:05:33,800 --> 00:05:35,800
soon to be weeblos.

103
00:05:35,800 --> 00:05:38,800
Well, I want to introduce the patrol method to my den.

104
00:05:38,800 --> 00:05:40,800
Do you think this is a good idea?

105
00:05:40,800 --> 00:05:44,800
And should I have the scouts divide themselves into patrols,

106
00:05:44,800 --> 00:05:46,800
Or should I divide them up?

107
00:05:46,800 --> 00:05:48,800
Maybe initially ask who wants to be a patrol leader?

108
00:05:48,800 --> 00:05:51,800
Well, Tim, some 30..

109
00:05:51,800 --> 00:05:53,800
Well, it's more than 30 years ago now.

110
00:05:53,800 --> 00:05:58,800
I had 18, count them 18- weeblos in my den.

111
00:05:58,800 --> 00:06:02,800
Thankfully, this was before the second year of weeblos was instituted

112
00:06:02,800 --> 00:06:04,800
And I was much younger. so I survived,

113
00:06:04,800 --> 00:06:07,800
As did almost all of the 18 weeblos,

114
00:06:07,800 --> 00:06:12,800
And I think, yes, definitely introduce the patrol method to your weeblos.

115
00:06:12,800 --> 00:06:15,800
But remember, you've been applying the patrol method as a den.

116
00:06:15,800 --> 00:06:17,800
You've just been the leader.

117
00:06:17,800 --> 00:06:20,800
So what you're really introducing to them is a little more autonomy

118
00:06:20,800 --> 00:06:24,800
and some responsibility for themselves and some decision making.

119
00:06:24,800 --> 00:06:27,800
So they will know exactly how a patrol works

120
00:06:27,800 --> 00:06:30,800
because you've been employing those methods as a den leader

121
00:06:30,800 --> 00:06:33,800
and they employ a lot of things in the patrol method

122
00:06:33,800 --> 00:06:35,800
all the time outside of scouts.

123
00:06:35,800 --> 00:06:37,800
They just don't know that that's what they're doing.

124
00:06:37,800 --> 00:06:41,800
So when you approach this, I would suggest maybe you think

125
00:06:41,800 --> 00:06:47,800
of how you would coach a team of this age of young people in basketball.

126
00:06:47,800 --> 00:06:51,800
Would you get them into the gym and explain everything about a layup

127
00:06:51,800 --> 00:06:54,800
in detail as they sat on the bench listening attentively?

128
00:06:54,800 --> 00:06:57,800
Or would you demonstrate a layup and then give them the ball

129
00:06:57,800 --> 00:06:59,800
so they could get at it right away?

130
00:06:59,800 --> 00:07:02,800
I'm going to guess you'd want them trying it out as soon as possible

131
00:07:02,800 --> 00:07:06,800
And I would use the same approach in introducing them to the idea

132
00:07:06,800 --> 00:07:10,800
of expanded independence and decision making

133
00:07:10,800 --> 00:07:13,800
and responsibility. so far as patrols are concerned,

134
00:07:13,800 --> 00:07:16,800
Don't explain it to them so much as show them how it works.

135
00:07:16,800 --> 00:07:19,800
Give them a simple 10-minute game or tasks to play

136
00:07:19,800 --> 00:07:21,800
or work on as a patrol. divide them up,

137
00:07:21,800 --> 00:07:23,800
point patrol leaders. let them at it.

138
00:07:23,800 --> 00:07:26,800
Once they've gone through the evolution of the game or tasks,

139
00:07:26,800 --> 00:07:28,800
do a two minute.

140
00:07:28,800 --> 00:07:30,800
When I say two minute, I'm talking literally two minutes.

141
00:07:30,800 --> 00:07:32,800
Use your watch.

142
00:07:32,800 --> 00:07:35,800
Do a two minute after action little session

143
00:07:35,800 --> 00:07:37,800
where you ask them how it works.

144
00:07:37,800 --> 00:07:39,800
Listen to their replies.

145
00:07:39,800 --> 00:07:41,800
Don't offer a whole lot in the way of advice.

146
00:07:41,800 --> 00:07:43,800
Just listen to what they have to say.

147
00:07:43,800 --> 00:07:46,800
Ask them more questions about how they think it could work better.

148
00:07:46,800 --> 00:07:49,800
Let them come up with those ideas on their own

149
00:07:49,800 --> 00:07:51,800
And then try it again.

150
00:07:51,800 --> 00:07:53,800
Now, sticking with the whole basketball analogy

151
00:07:53,800 --> 00:07:57,800
now, would you have your brand new team play a league game

152
00:07:57,800 --> 00:07:59,800
the first time that they were out together?

153
00:07:59,800 --> 00:08:00,800
Think about that one.

154
00:08:00,800 --> 00:08:02,800
Would you explain things about the game

155
00:08:02,800 --> 00:08:03,800
and show them the rule book

156
00:08:03,800 --> 00:08:06,800
and then throw them out to the court with the other team?

157
00:08:06,800 --> 00:08:07,800
I know

158
00:08:07,800 --> 00:08:09,800
I mean the answer is obvious. right

159
00:08:09,800 --> 00:08:10,800
The same with patrols.

160
00:08:10,800 --> 00:08:13,800
Don't make a big deal out of establishing permanent,

161
00:08:13,800 --> 00:08:14,800
everlasting patrols

162
00:08:14,800 --> 00:08:17,800
and trying to explain every little detail about it

163
00:08:17,800 --> 00:08:18,800
before you start.

164
00:08:18,800 --> 00:08:20,800
In other words, don't take your den

165
00:08:20,800 --> 00:08:22,800
and just push them off into the deep end

166
00:08:22,800 --> 00:08:24,800
and say: okay, now you're in patrols, have a good time.

167
00:08:24,800 --> 00:08:26,800
Try the approach that I explain,

168
00:08:26,800 --> 00:08:28,800
You know. just set them up in two different groups,

169
00:08:28,800 --> 00:08:30,800
appoint a leader, give them a game or a task

170
00:08:30,800 --> 00:08:32,799
That's going to last a few minutes,

171
00:08:32,799 --> 00:08:34,799
Then ask them some questions afterwards

172
00:08:34,799 --> 00:08:36,799
and take your time with it.

173
00:08:36,799 --> 00:08:37,799
Go slow,

174
00:08:37,799 --> 00:08:38,799
Don't attempt to explain everything.

175
00:08:38,799 --> 00:08:40,799
Let them figure it out a little bit at a time.

176
00:08:40,799 --> 00:08:42,799
You'll see some positive things happening

177
00:08:42,799 --> 00:08:43,799
and some negative things.

178
00:08:43,799 --> 00:08:46,800
I would do your best to ignore the negative things

179
00:08:46,800 --> 00:08:49,800
and follow all over yourself about the positive things.

180
00:08:49,800 --> 00:08:51,800
Every time you praise one of the positive things

181
00:08:51,800 --> 00:08:52,800
that you see happening,

182
00:08:52,800 --> 00:08:54,800
apply a point of the scout law

183
00:08:54,800 --> 00:08:57,800
to define the positive thing you have seen.

184
00:08:57,800 --> 00:09:00,800
An immutable rule of scouting at this age

185
00:09:00,800 --> 00:09:01,800
and really at all ages,

186
00:09:01,800 --> 00:09:04,800
is that you'll perpetuate what you pay attention to.

187
00:09:04,800 --> 00:09:06,800
Make a big deal out of negative behavior,

188
00:09:06,800 --> 00:09:07,800
and it will be repeated.

189
00:09:07,800 --> 00:09:09,800
but deal with it quickly and quietly,

190
00:09:09,800 --> 00:09:11,800
and it will be less likely to recur.

191
00:09:11,800 --> 00:09:13,800
Make a big deal out of positive behavior,

192
00:09:13,800 --> 00:09:15,800
and it too will be repeated.

193
00:09:15,800 --> 00:09:17,800
I would say that in my experience,

194
00:09:17,800 --> 00:09:19,800
most Weeblows den leaders

195
00:09:19,800 --> 00:09:21,800
overestimate the abilities of their scouts,

196
00:09:21,800 --> 00:09:23,800
at least a little too much.

197
00:09:23,800 --> 00:09:25,800
They go a little too far

198
00:09:25,800 --> 00:09:27,800
with the sophistication of activities

199
00:09:27,800 --> 00:09:29,800
and, like most adults,

200
00:09:29,800 --> 00:09:31,800
they talk way too much.

201
00:09:31,800 --> 00:09:33,800
You have to have some game, to be

202
00:09:33,800 --> 00:09:35,800
a good Weeblows den leader

203
00:09:35,800 --> 00:09:37,800
and your challenge is leading them

204
00:09:37,800 --> 00:09:39,800
through a very active hour

205
00:09:39,800 --> 00:09:40,800
of time every week

206
00:09:40,800 --> 00:09:43,800
where you talk or stop the action

207
00:09:43,800 --> 00:09:44,800
as little as possible.

208
00:09:44,800 --> 00:09:45,800
That should be your goal,

209
00:09:45,800 --> 00:09:47,800
And one of the things that's really on your side,

210
00:09:47,800 --> 00:09:48,800
as I said,

211
00:09:48,800 --> 00:09:50,800
is they already know how this works,

212
00:09:50,800 --> 00:09:52,800
even though they don't know that. they know

213
00:09:52,800 --> 00:09:54,800
and they'll catch on very quickly.

214
00:09:54,800 --> 00:09:56,800
Don't make it a system,

215
00:09:56,800 --> 00:09:57,800
Make it a method.

216
00:09:57,800 --> 00:09:59,800
I've pointed this out a few times recently.

217
00:09:59,800 --> 00:10:01,800
talking about the system and the method,

218
00:10:01,800 --> 00:10:03,800
Don't worry about how the patrols are made up

219
00:10:03,800 --> 00:10:05,800
and don't get overzealous

220
00:10:05,800 --> 00:10:07,800
about those,

221
00:10:07,800 --> 00:10:09,800
about the systemic and administrative things.

222
00:10:09,800 --> 00:10:12,800
Look at how the scouts are behaving

223
00:10:12,800 --> 00:10:13,800
and how they're working together.

224
00:10:13,800 --> 00:10:14,800
Observe a lot,

225
00:10:14,800 --> 00:10:15,800
Listen a lot.

226
00:10:15,800 --> 00:10:17,800
You'll see who works well together

227
00:10:17,800 --> 00:10:18,800
and who doesn't.

228
00:10:18,800 --> 00:10:19,800
and you know,

229
00:10:19,800 --> 00:10:21,800
if you've already been with these guys for a year,

230
00:10:21,800 --> 00:10:23,800
you got a pretty good idea of that. now

231
00:10:23,800 --> 00:10:25,800
stay away from having them

232
00:10:25,800 --> 00:10:27,800
make the choices of who's going to be

233
00:10:27,800 --> 00:10:28,800
and what patrol now,

234
00:10:28,800 --> 00:10:30,800
because there's just a whole lot of baggage

235
00:10:30,800 --> 00:10:31,800
that goes with that,

236
00:10:31,800 --> 00:10:33,800
especially at this age.

237
00:10:33,800 --> 00:10:34,800
and you know-

238
00:10:34,800 --> 00:10:36,800
and I need to qualify

239
00:10:36,800 --> 00:10:38,800
that advice by saying

240
00:10:38,800 --> 00:10:40,800
not every group is the same.

241
00:10:40,800 --> 00:10:42,800
So you know, they may very well be able

242
00:10:42,800 --> 00:10:44,800
to form their own patrols equitably

243
00:10:44,800 --> 00:10:46,800
and make those good choices,

244
00:10:46,800 --> 00:10:48,800
but I don't know.

245
00:10:48,800 --> 00:10:51,800
I would probably recommend that you set it up

246
00:10:51,800 --> 00:10:53,800
and appoint the patrol leader

247
00:10:53,800 --> 00:10:55,800
for a meeting at a time

248
00:10:55,800 --> 00:10:56,800
and then, you know,

249
00:10:56,800 --> 00:10:57,800
switch things up,

250
00:10:57,800 --> 00:10:58,800
try various combinations

251
00:10:58,800 --> 00:11:00,800
and talk with your scouts about it a lot.

252
00:11:00,800 --> 00:11:02,800
what you want in the end

253
00:11:02,800 --> 00:11:05,800
is weeblows who understand how patrol works

254
00:11:05,800 --> 00:11:07,800
and their responsibility

255
00:11:07,800 --> 00:11:08,800
in making it work.

256
00:11:08,800 --> 00:11:09,800
they'll get some things right away.

257
00:11:09,800 --> 00:11:11,800
some things will be small,

258
00:11:11,800 --> 00:11:13,800
incremental changes over the next couple of years.

259
00:11:13,800 --> 00:11:14,800
and remember,

260
00:11:14,800 --> 00:11:16,800
your position is called

261
00:11:16,800 --> 00:11:18,800
weeblows den leader

262
00:11:18,800 --> 00:11:19,800
for a reason.

263
00:11:19,800 --> 00:11:21,800
scouts this age are not quite ready

264
00:11:21,800 --> 00:11:23,800
to make complex leadership decisions

265
00:11:23,800 --> 00:11:25,800
and lead each other

